# Important Research Papers

## Research Topic
**Evaluating the Cross-Attack Generalization of Adversarial Training in ML-Based Intrusion Detection**

This document summarises important research papers related to the proposed study. The selected papers provide background on adversarial machine learning, FGSM, JSMA, DeepFool, intrusion detection, CIC-IDS2017, and adversarial robustness. They also help identify the research gap concerning the ability of adversarial training to generalise from a seen attack to unseen attacks.

---

## Research Paper 1

### Title
Explaining and Harnessing Adversarial Examples

### Authors
Ian J. Goodfellow, Jonathon Shlens, and Christian Szegedy

### Year
2015

### Research Problem
The study investigated why machine-learning models are vulnerable to adversarial examples. The researchers examined how small and intentionally designed changes to input data could cause a trained model to make incorrect predictions.

### Method / Technique
The researchers introduced the Fast Gradient Sign Method (FGSM), which uses gradient information from the model to generate adversarial examples. The study also explored adversarial training by including adversarial examples during the training process.

### Dataset / Tools
The experiments mainly involved image-classification datasets, including MNIST, together with neural-network models.

### Main Findings
The study demonstrated that relatively small adversarial perturbations can cause machine-learning models to produce incorrect predictions. FGSM was shown to be a simple and efficient method for generating adversarial examples. The research also showed that using adversarial examples during training can improve model robustness.

### Limitations
The study mainly focused on image classification and neural networks rather than cybersecurity or network intrusion detection. It also did not specifically investigate whether robustness learned from FGSM could generalise to different adversarial attacks such as JSMA and DeepFool.

### Relevance to Our Research
This paper provides an important foundation for our research because FGSM is used to generate the adversarial examples for adversarial training. Our study extends this idea by investigating whether FGSM-based adversarial training can also improve robustness against unseen attacks such as JSMA and DeepFool.

### Reference
Goodfellow, I. J., Shlens, J., & Szegedy, C. (2015). Explaining and harnessing adversarial examples. *International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR).*

