## Research Paper 2

### Title
The Limitations of Deep Learning in Adversarial Settings

### Authors
Nicolas Papernot, Patrick McDaniel, Somesh Jha, Matt Fredrikson, Z. Berkay Celik, and Ananthram Swami

### Year
2016

### Research Problem
The study investigated the vulnerability of machine-learning models to adversarial manipulation. The researchers examined whether carefully selected changes to input features could cause a model to produce an incorrect classification.

### Method / Technique
The researchers proposed a method based on the model's Jacobian and a saliency map, commonly known as the Jacobian-based Saliency Map Attack (JSMA). The technique identifies input features that strongly influence the model's output and selectively modifies them.

### Dataset / Tools
The experiments mainly focused on deep-learning and image-classification environments.

### Main Findings
The study demonstrated that machine-learning models could be successfully manipulated by modifying selected influential input features. The findings showed that an attacker does not necessarily need to modify every feature to produce an adversarial example.

### Limitations
The study mainly focused on deep-learning and image-classification applications rather than ML-based network intrusion detection. It did not evaluate CIC-IDS2017, Logistic Regression, or cross-attack generalisation after FGSM adversarial training.

### Relevance to Our Research
JSMA is one of the unseen attacks selected for our experiment. Since JSMA generates adversarial examples differently from FGSM, it provides an appropriate way to test whether the robustness learned through FGSM adversarial training can generalise to another attack technique.

### Reference
Papernot, N., McDaniel, P., Jha, S., Fredrikson, M., Celik, Z. B., & Swami, A. (2016). The limitations of deep learning in adversarial settings. *2016 IEEE European Symposium on Security and Privacy*, 372–387. https://doi.org/10.1109/EuroSP.2016.36

