## Research Paper 3

### Title
DeepFool: A Simple and Accurate Method to Fool Deep Neural Networks

### Authors
Seyed-Mohsen Moosavi-Dezfooli, Alhussein Fawzi, and Pascal Frossard

### Year
2016

### Research Problem
The researchers investigated how to generate relatively small adversarial perturbations that are sufficient to change the prediction of a classifier.

### Method / Technique
The study introduced DeepFool, an iterative adversarial attack. DeepFool attempts to estimate the closest decision boundary of a classifier and gradually modifies an input until the model changes its prediction.

### Dataset / Tools
The experiments mainly used image-classification datasets and deep neural-network models.

### Main Findings
The study demonstrated that DeepFool can efficiently generate relatively small perturbations that successfully change model classifications. The technique also provides a useful approach for analysing the robustness of machine-learning classifiers.

### Limitations
The original study was mainly focused on image classification and deep neural networks. It did not specifically investigate ML-based intrusion detection, CIC-IDS2017, Logistic Regression, or cross-attack generalisation.

### Relevance to Our Research
DeepFool is used as the second unseen attack in our research. Its attack mechanism differs from FGSM because it focuses on crossing the model's decision boundary. Testing the FGSM-trained model against DeepFool will help determine whether the learned robustness can generalise to a different adversarial strategy.

### Reference
Moosavi-Dezfooli, S.-M., Fawzi, A., & Frossard, P. (2016). DeepFool: A simple and accurate method to fool deep neural networks. *2016 IEEE Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition*, 2574–2582. https://doi.org/10.1109/CVPR.2016.282

