## Research Paper 4

### Title
Toward Generating a New Intrusion Detection Dataset and Intrusion Traffic Characterization

### Authors
Iman Sharafaldin, Arash Habibi Lashkari, and Ali A. Ghorbani

### Year
2018

### Research Problem
The study addressed limitations in existing intrusion-detection datasets. The researchers aimed to develop a dataset containing realistic benign and malicious network traffic that could be used to evaluate intrusion-detection systems.

### Method / Technique
A controlled network environment was developed to generate normal user behaviour together with different attack scenarios. Network traffic was captured and converted into flow-based features for intrusion-detection experiments.

### Dataset / Tools
CIC-IDS2017 and CICFlowMeter were used to generate and extract network-flow information.

### Main Findings
The study produced the CIC-IDS2017 dataset, which contains labelled benign and malicious network traffic. The dataset includes different attack scenarios and network-flow features that can be used for machine-learning-based intrusion-detection research.

### Limitations
The dataset was collected within a controlled environment and during a specific period. Therefore, it may not fully represent every type of network behaviour or newly emerging cyberattack found in real-world environments.

### Relevance to Our Research
This paper is directly relevant because CIC-IDS2017 is the dataset selected for our experiment. The dataset will be preprocessed and used to train and evaluate the Logistic Regression-based intrusion detection model before and after adversarial training.

### Reference
Sharafaldin, I., Habibi Lashkari, A., & Ghorbani, A. A. (2018). Toward generating a new intrusion detection dataset and intrusion traffic characterization. *Proceedings of the 4th International Conference on Information Systems Security and Privacy (ICISSP).*

