## Research Paper 5

### Title
Adversarial Attacks Against Supervised Machine Learning Based Network Intrusion Detection Systems

### Authors
A. U. H. Qureshi, H. Larijani, J. Ahmad, and N. Mtetwa

### Year
2022

### Research Problem
The study investigated the vulnerability of supervised machine-learning-based network intrusion detection systems to adversarial attacks. The researchers examined how adversarial manipulation could reduce the ability of ML classifiers to correctly identify malicious network activities.

### Method / Technique
The study investigated adversarial attacks against supervised machine-learning classifiers. The experiments included Logistic Regression and Decision Tree classifiers and considered adversarial scenarios affecting ML-based intrusion detection.

### Dataset / Tools
The study used the CIC-IDS2017 dataset together with tools and libraries including Python, scikit-learn, Pandas, NumPy, Matplotlib, and TensorFlow.

### Main Findings
The results demonstrated that adversarial manipulation can reduce the performance of supervised ML-based intrusion detection systems. The findings highlight that achieving good classification performance on normal network data does not automatically mean that an ML model is robust against adversarial attacks.

### Limitations
The study did not specifically investigate the cross-attack generalisation of FGSM-based adversarial training. In particular, it did not use the exact experimental setup of training a Logistic Regression defense using FGSM and subsequently testing it against FGSM, JSMA, and DeepFool.

### Relevance to Our Research
This paper is highly relevant because it investigates adversarial attacks against ML-based network intrusion detection and uses both CIC-IDS2017 and Logistic Regression. These are also key components of our research. The study supports the need to evaluate the adversarial robustness of Logistic Regression-based intrusion detection models.

### Reference
Qureshi, A. U. H., Larijani, H., Ahmad, J., & Mtetwa, N. (2022). Adversarial attacks against supervised machine learning based network intrusion detection systems. *PLOS ONE, 17*(10), e0275971. https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0275971

