# Overall Comparison and Relevance

The reviewed papers provide the main foundation for the proposed research. Goodfellow et al. (2015), Papernot et al. (2016), and Moosavi-Dezfooli et al. (2016) establish the adversarial attack techniques used in the experiment: FGSM, JSMA, and DeepFool. Although these attacks share the objective of causing incorrect model predictions, they use different methods to generate adversarial examples.

Sharafaldin et al. (2018) provides the foundation for the CIC-IDS2017 dataset, while Qureshi et al. (2022) demonstrates that supervised ML-based intrusion detection models, including Logistic Regression, can be vulnerable to adversarial manipulation. Verma et al. (2025) further shows that defensive effectiveness can vary between different adversarial attacks.

Together, these studies show that adversarial training can be useful for improving model robustness, but they also highlight an important issue: successful defense against one adversarial attack does not necessarily guarantee protection against another.

Therefore, our research focuses specifically on cross-attack generalisation. FGSM will be used to train the adversarial defense and will also be used as the seen attack during testing. JSMA and DeepFool will only be introduced during testing and will therefore represent unseen attacks. The comparison will determine whether FGSM-based adversarial training produces robustness mainly against FGSM or whether that robustness can generalise to different adversarial attack techniques.

---

# Research Gap

Previous research has demonstrated the vulnerability of machine-learning models to adversarial attacks and the ability of adversarial training to improve robustness. However, there is still limited evidence regarding whether a defense trained using one adversarial attack can maintain its robustness when exposed to different attacks that were not included during training.

This creates the research gap addressed by the proposed study:

**There is limited empirical evidence on whether FGSM-based adversarial training in an ML-based intrusion detection system can generalise its robustness to unseen adversarial attacks such as JSMA and DeepFool.**

The proposed experiment addresses this gap using CIC-IDS2017 and Logistic Regression. FGSM is used for adversarial training, while the defended model is tested against FGSM as the seen attack and JSMA and DeepFool as unseen attacks. The results will be compared to determine the level of cross-attack generalisation achieved by the defense.
